2.5.4.m:
Bring to us, Indra, radiant good fortune;
may we abide in the surplus of your gift;
like an abyss, desire has expanded in us;
fill that, O lord of possessions, with goods.
Delight this desire with cows and horses,
and expand it with brilliant reward;
seeking heaven through their thoughts for you, the sages,
the Kuśikas have made a vehicle for Indra.
Now I will proclaim the heroic deeds of Indra,
which the vajra-wielder performed as the first ones.

He slew the serpent; he drilled through following the waters;
he split open the bellies of the mountains.
He slew the serpent lying upon the mountain;
Tvaṣṭṛ fashioned for him the heavenly vajra;
like lowing cows flowing,
the waters went straight down to the sea.
Acting like a bull, he chose the soma;
he drank of the pressed [soma] in the Trikadruka [rites];
the generous one took the vajra missile;
he slew him, the first-born of the serpents.

When, O Indra, you slew the first-born of the serpents,
then you confounded the illusions of the illusionists,
and then, bringing forth the sun, the sky, the dawn,
truly then you found no enemies at all.
Indra slew Vṛtra, the greater vṛtra, with shoulders crushed,
with his vajra, the great weapon;
like tree trunks hewn apart with an axe,
the serpent lay clinging to the earth.
For, intoxicated, he challenged [Indra] as if unable to fight,
to the great hero, the conqueror, the drinker of soma dregs.

2.5.5.m:
O goddess of the forest, O goddess of the forest yonder, / you who seem to vanish away: / how is it that you do not ask for the village? / Does not fear, as it were, overcome you?
When the ciccika bird answers / the crying cricket, / as if rushing with cymbals, / the goddess of the forest is glorified.
And it seems as if cattle are eating, / and it seems as if a dwelling is seen;

2.5.6.m:
Auspicious to you be both these, heaven and earth;
auspicious to you be Agni together with the waters; / auspicious to you be the four quarters.
The divine four quarters / whose lord is the wind, upon which the sun looks: / to them I commit you until old age; / let consumption depart far off to Nirṛti! / He has been released from consumption, from evil, from distress;

2.5.7.m:
For a long lifespan, for one of a hundred autumns,
I accept [it], for great heroic power.
You are lifespan, you are all lifespan,
you are complete lifespan, you are entire lifespan.
May my entire lifespan be fulfilled;
may I attain my entire lifespan.
Bhūḥ! Bhuvaḥ! Suvaḥ!
Agni by righteousness is the eater of food;
Death by righteousness is the lord of food;
brahman, regal power, svāhā!

Prajāpati is the guide;
Bṛhaspati is the vanguard;
Yama is the path;
the Moon is the reviver of breath, svāhā!
Agni is the eater of food, the lord of food;
may he give food to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Soma is king, the lord of kings;
may he give kingship to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Varuṇa is the sovereign, the lord of sovereigns;
may he give sovereignty to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!

Mitra is regal power, the lord of regal power;
may he give regal power to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Indra is strength, the lord of strength;
may he give strength to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Bṛhaspati is brahman, the lord of brahman;
may he give brahman to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Savitṛ is dominion, the lord of dominion;
may he give dominion to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Pūṣan is the lord of the clans of the clans; may he give the clan to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Sarasvatī is nourishment, the mistress of nourishment;
may she give nourishment to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Tvaṣṭṛ, the maker of forms for pairs of domestic animals, is the lord of forms;
may he give domestic animals in [proper] form to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!

2.5.8.m:
Drink this, you who are residueless, victorious,
who are full-cheeked, a bull among the inspired,
who are breaker of pens, wielder of the thunderbolt, who stand on bay horses;
O Indra, open up wondrous spoils of victory.
May your bull-like fury come from behind,
from the north, from below, from the front;
may it assemble toward us from all sides;
O Indra, bestow on us heavenly splendor.
Advance toward him, in front of the chariot;
sing of fury to Indra!

O Maruts, [sing] to the foremost slayer of Vṛtra,
with whom the increasers of truth produced light,
the watchful god for the god.
What are these drizzles, what are these winged insects?
Locusts swarm around me in a flock;
may the gods blow them away unimpeded;
may I obtain the eye of an eagle in my body.
Thus praise the lofty Varuṇa,
reverence the wise guardian of immortality;
may he extend to us threefold shelter.

grant to me from that;
may I partake of that of yours;
may we become the greatest sharers in that of yours.
I am from you, you are from me in this;
you are my womb, I am your womb;
being mine, O Agni, convey the oblations,
as a son creating wide space for his father, O Jātavedas.
Being right here, while you are there, O Agni,
I sustain you with exhalation, speech, and mind;
while I am hidden, may lifespan not abandon me.

I attend you with the light of Vaiśvānara.
This is your seasonable womb,
from which born you shone;
knowing it, O Agni, mount it,
and then increase our wealth.
With that body of yours that is worthy of sacrifice, O Agni, come, mount your self,
preparing many manly deeds for us;
having become the sacrifice, sit upon the sacrifice, your own womb;
O Jātavedas, being born of the earth, come with your dwelling!
Step down again, O Jātavedas!

2.6.14.m:
The divine Indra, the tree of the forest,
golden-leaved for the two Aśvins,
with fine berries for Sarasvatī;
for Indra, the sweet drink is cooked.
Like splendorous might, speed; like a bull, fury;
may the tree of the forest establish indriyas for us.
For the winning of wealth, let them partake of the gift of wealth; sacrifice!
The divine sacred grass of the waters,
strewn at the sacrificial rite for the two Aśvins,
soft as wool for Sarasvatī;

A pleasant seat for you, O Indra;
they established wrath for the sovereign, the king, indriya through the sacred grass.
For the winning of wealth, let them partake of the gift of wealth; sacrifice!
May the divine Agni Sviṣṭakṛt
sacrifice to the gods according to their order;
the two Hotṛs, the two Aśvins for Indra,
with speech, speech to Sarasvatī,
Agni, Soma, the Sviṣṭakṛt;
well-sacrificed is Indra Sutrāman, Savitṛ, Varuṇa the physician;
sacrificed to is the divine tree of the forest;
well-sacrificed are the gods who drink clarified butter;
sacrificed to is Agni by Agni;
the Hotṛ, the Sviṣṭakṛt for the priest,
establishing indriya like renown,
vigor, reverence, the svadhā-call.
For the winning of wealth, let them partake of the gift of wealth; sacrifice!

2.6.16.m:
The Fathers, releasing from distress, devoted to soma,
becoming immortal, both higher and lower,
may they speak in our favor, may they protect us,
enjoying the porridge mixed with the milk of the newly calved cow;
Arising, the lower and the higher,
consumed by fire, in agreement with the seasons,
accompanied by Indra, may they enjoy this oblation.
When, O Agni, bearer of ancestral oblations; you, O Agni, praised, O Jātavedas;
Mātali with the ancestral offerings;
who, yearning among the gods, took delight;
thriving on the priest's call, fashioned through praise-songs with sacred chants;
Come hither, O Agni, with the benevolent,
true Fathers, eaters of the kavya, seated at the hot cauldron;
Serving Agni, the conveyor of oblations, ageless, dear to many,
with clarified butter, with the offering,
I have approached the ancestral oblation-bearer of the Fathers;
May he unite us with offspring rich in heroes!

2.7.16.m:
All songs have magnified Indra, / wide as the sea, / the greatest charioteer among charioteers, / the true lord, the master of prizes.
May the sounds of the army / and the greedy bowstrings bypass me; / standing at the post, swelling here, / let them enter into me, the lord of cattle.
May Anumati agree to that for me; / may mother earth and father heaven agree to that;

2.8.3.m:
May Soma give a milch cow, may Soma [give] a swift steed;
may Soma give a heroic son, skillful in action,
fit for the home, fit for the assembly, fit for the council,
bringing fame to his father, who serves him.
[The pratīkas:] "Unconquered in battles," "You, O Soma, by your insights,"
"Those forms of yours they sacrifice to with the oblation."
You, O Soma, [created] all these plants;
you brought forth the waters, you the cattle;
you extended the broad intermediate space;
you uncovered the darkness with light.

Everything firm trembles, shaken by him;
the waters flowed in accordance with his own nature.
He grew in the midst of navigable waters;
with a concentrated mind, O Indra, with the mightiest
blow you struck him through the days.
Accompanied by the Maruts, the bull growing great,
not withholding, the heavenly commander, Indra,
all-conquering, for new aid,
that mighty giver of strength let us summon here;
he was born mighty for swift power.
